If you design with Canva and want less AI in your workspace, the answer from Canva's own help page is that there are now two different settings, and they do very different jobs. This post explains what the page says, what it leaves out, and what you should check in your own account before relying on either one.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, from Canva's published help page. Stackbrook did not test Canva's settings and has no affiliate relationship with Canva at the time of writing. Because only one page could be read in full, this draft did not receive the independent AI review we normally apply, which is why it is held back rather than published. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not legal or privacy advice.*

## The two settings, in plain terms

The first is an account-wide switch for AI features. Canva's page says it lets you turn its AI features off in one place instead of adjusting each feature separately. It applies to Canva Free and Canva Pro accounts. The AI features are on by default, so nothing changes unless you flip it.

The second is a content filter called "Show less AI-generated templates and elements". It does not switch any tool off. It only changes what appears when you search or browse recommendations, nudging results away from items Canva has tagged as AI-generated. It is off by default and affects only your own account.

Canva says both are rolling out gradually, so you may not see them yet. That is the first thing to check: if the option is missing from your account, it is probably not a mistake on your side.

![Two-column diagram comparing Canva's AI features switch with the show-less-AI-content filter](/images/site1/canva-ai-off-switch-what-it-turns-off-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

## What the main switch covers

According to the help page, the switch covers AI features that already have a permission setting. New AI features will follow the setting once Canva adds them, which means a feature launched next month should respect a switch you set today. Some low-risk features, with background remover given as the example, stay available whether the switch is on or off.

Two limits are worth noting. The setting does not carry over if your account moves between Free or Pro and a Teams, Enterprise or Education plan, in either direction, so you would need to check it again after a change. And the page does not list every feature the switch controls, so "off" should not be read as a guarantee that nothing AI-assisted can happen in your designs.

## What the filter does and does not do

The filter has a menu path on the page: open your profile icon, choose your account, open the AI personalization section, and turn off the AI-generated content option. Canva states several limits. Some AI-generated content may still appear, because the filter depends on how well Canva's own flagging catches it. It does not label content as AI-generated, it does not change AI content you have already placed in a design, and it does not affect Brand Templates or searches of your own uploads and designs. At the time of the page, it was available in the Global market only.

If your goal is to keep AI-looking stock templates out of your search results, this is the setting aimed at that. If your goal is to stop AI tools from being used in your account, it is not the right one.

## Where the help page stops

Several things a business owner would want to know are not on the page that was read. The steps for the main switch are marked as coming soon, so there is no confirmed menu path yet. The page says nothing about how your content is used for AI training or what happens to data when a feature is off. And for team accounts, the page only points to a separate article about changing access to Magic and AI features, which could not be fetched in this run, so who can change settings for a team and how is not confirmed here.

A reasonable next step for a team is to read that separate admin article directly and note the date you checked it, since the wording on rollout pages tends to change as features reach everyone.

## A quick checklist

Check whether the switch and the filter appear in your account. If a team manages your Canva, ask the admin which setting applies. If you handle client or customer material, confirm Canva's data terms yourself rather than assuming an off switch changes them. Treat anything beyond Canva's own help pages, including news coverage of the launch, as unconfirmed until you can see Canva's own announcement.

Canva's page gives no date, so this post cannot say when the settings first became available. Checked on 21 November 2026, the facts above are only as current as that page.
